steps.brief:
UPDATE changes values in the rows that pass WHERE. Check those rows first with a SELECT:

~~~sql
SELECT id, camera, seen_at, plate FROM camera_sightings WHERE plate LIKE 'HC-1%';
~~~

Then change only them:

~~~sql
UPDATE vehicles SET color = 'dark grey' WHERE plate = 'HC-1065';
SELECT plate, color FROM vehicles WHERE plate = 'HC-1065';
~~~

A common mistake is <code>SET plate * 2</code> or forgetting the <code>=</code>: SET always reads column = new value. Several columns: <code>SET a = 1, b = 2</code>.

DELETE removes the rows that pass WHERE. Look first:

~~~sql
SELECT id, badge_no, door, scanned_at, source, entered_by FROM badge_scans WHERE source = 'manual';
~~~

Front desks can type a scan by hand when someone forgot their badge. Each manual scan records who typed it in <code>entered_by</code>. Then delete exactly those rows, with the same WHERE:

~~~sql
DELETE FROM badge_scans WHERE entered_by = 'desk.anne';
SELECT COUNT(*) AS manual_left FROM badge_scans WHERE source = 'manual';
~~~

