steps.brief:
A trigger is SQL that runs by itself when a table changes. It is perfect for an audit log nobody can forget to fill.

### An AFTER INSERT trigger with NEW

~~~sql
CREATE TABLE statement_alerts (incident_id INTEGER, witness TEXT);
CREATE TRIGGER alert_new_statement
AFTER INSERT ON witness_statements
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO statement_alerts VALUES (NEW.incident_id, NEW.witness);
END;
INSERT INTO witness_statements (incident_id, witness) VALUES (8, 'Anonymous caller');
SELECT * FROM statement_alerts;
~~~

- AFTER INSERT ON table: when it fires. BEFORE also exists: it runs before the change.
- BEGIN ... END;: the statements to run, each ending with a semicolon.
- NEW.column: the new row's values.

### OLD: the row before the change

In a DELETE trigger only OLD exists; in an INSERT trigger only NEW. UPDATE triggers have both.

~~~sql
CREATE TABLE deleted_scans (badge_no TEXT, scanned_at TEXT);
CREATE TRIGGER keep_deleted_scans
AFTER DELETE ON badge_scans
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO deleted_scans VALUES (OLD.badge_no, OLD.scanned_at);
END;
DELETE FROM badge_scans WHERE source = 'manual';
SELECT * FROM deleted_scans;
~~~

### UPDATE OF a column, and WHEN

<code>AFTER UPDATE OF status</code> fires only when that column is in the SET. <code>WHEN condition</code>, written between the event line and BEGIN, fires only if the test is true: <code>OLD.status &lt;&gt; NEW.status</code> means "really changed". <code>DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> stamps a column with the current date and time.

~~~sql
CREATE TABLE status_log (incident_id INTEGER, old_status TEXT, new_status TEXT, logged_at TEXT DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);
CREATE TRIGGER log_status
AFTER UPDATE OF status ON incidents
WHEN OLD.status <> NEW.status
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO status_log (incident_id, old_status, new_status) VALUES (NEW.id, OLD.status, NEW.status);
END;
UPDATE incidents SET status = 'closed' WHERE id = 3;
UPDATE incidents SET status = status WHERE id = 4;
SELECT incident_id, old_status, new_status FROM status_log;
~~~

Only one row: the second UPDATE changed nothing.

### Listing and removing triggers

~~~sql
CREATE TRIGGER noop AFTER INSERT ON accounts BEGIN SELECT 1; END;
SELECT type, name, tbl_name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'trigger';
~~~

<code>DROP TRIGGER name;</code> removes one. Common mistakes: a missing <code>END;</code>, WHEN placed after BEGIN, and using <code>=</code> when you mean "changed".

