steps.brief:
A transaction groups statements so that they all happen, or none do.

### BEGIN and COMMIT

~~~sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE evidence_items SET locker = 'L-6' WHERE id = 7;
INSERT INTO evidence_items (incident_id, item, locker) VALUES (12, 'Second jerrycan', 'L-6');
COMMIT;
SELECT id, item, locker FROM evidence_items WHERE locker = 'L-6';
~~~

Between BEGIN and COMMIT, other users do not see half the work. COMMIT makes it permanent.

### ROLLBACK: undo everything since BEGIN

~~~sql
BEGIN;
DELETE FROM evidence_items;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS during_transaction FROM evidence_items;
ROLLBACK;
SELECT COUNT(*) AS after_rollback FROM evidence_items;
~~~

### The classic example: a transfer

Money must leave one account and arrive in the other, never just one of the two. Here the recovered 4860 goes back from the evidence safe to Harbor Market:

~~~sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 4860 WHERE owner = 'HCPD evidence safe';
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 4860 WHERE owner = 'Harbor Market';
COMMIT;
SELECT * FROM accounts;
~~~

### Several transactions in one script, and the rules

One script can hold several transactions: each BEGIN is closed by its own COMMIT or ROLLBACK. Rules: BEGIN cannot be nested; a ROLLBACK after COMMIT undoes nothing; a transaction left open is lost when the connection closes.

